    "result_text": "The build is green. Here's a summary of what I implemented.\n\n## Summary\n\nMoved all date-derived values to a **fiscal-year basis** (fiscal year starts 1 July, named by the calendar year it ends in \u2014 e.g. 1 Jul 2025\u201330 Jun 2026 is fiscal year 2026 \u2192 `FY26`).\n\n**New shared helper** \u2014 `OwnerFiscalYear.java` (in the owner function package, alongside the existing `OwnerRegion`/`OwnerIdentity` utilities): a single source of truth with `fiscalYearOf`, `fiscalYearLabel` (`FY<YY>`), and `elapsedFiscalYears`. All derivations read off the owner's **business-day-adjusted** registration date (set by `NormalizeOwnerRegistrationDate`).\n\n**Changes:**\n1. **`fiscalYear` field** \u2014 added to the `Owner` schema in `openapi.yml` (regenerates the DTO) and mapped in `OwnerMapper.fiscalYear(owner)` \u2192 returns `FY<YY>`. This satisfies the new `coreReturnsFiscalYear` acceptance test (`FY\\d{2}`).\n2. **Membership identifier** (`OwnerMapper.membershipNumber`) \u2014 the `-M<YY>` year segment now uses the fiscal year instead of the raw registration-date calendar year (behaviour change: e.g. registration 2026-10-05 \u2192 `...-M27`, since October 2026 is fiscal year 2027).\n3. **Tenure** (`AssignMembershipLevel`) \u2014 now counted as **elapsed fiscal years** (`fiscalYearOf(now) \u2212 fiscalYearOf(registrationDate)`) with the 3-point threshold at \"more than one elapsed fiscal year\", replacing the old 365-day window. A newly created owner still has zero tenure, so it never earns the tenure points.\n4. Updated the openapi descriptions for `membershipNumber` and `membershipPoints` to match.\n\n`./mvnw -o test` \u2192 **BUILD SUCCESS**, Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0.\n\nNote: I chose the end-year fiscal-year convention (1 Jul 2025\u201330 Jun 2026 = FY26) and applied it consistently across the fiscal year label, membership identifier, and tenure count \u2014 the spec fixes the 1 July start but not the naming, and this is the common convention. Easy to flip to start-year naming if you'd prefer.",
